WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.760
ai just beat licensed accountants at real-work tasks, and you can use the same trick to get better at anything. This is Models &amp; Agents for Beginners, Episode 184. Mercore reports that researchers hired 12 licensed junior accountants. Each person got realistic month-end work, the cleanup companies do when a month closes. That meant digging through company files, doing the math, and making clean tables of results. Today's top ai models received the exact same jobs.

00:00:29.760 --> 00:00:35.000
jobs. When the jobs were clearly defined, the models were quicker. They also made fewer

00:00:35.000 --> 00:00:37.680
mistakes than the best accountant in that group of 12.

00:00:38.420 --> 00:00:43.580
18 months before this test, the strongest models still sat well below the average accountant.

00:00:44.120 --> 00:00:49.540
The leap came from a big jump in following detailed instructions. Patterns bought in numbers

00:00:49.540 --> 00:00:55.200
and documents got much sharper too. Picture packing your locker on the last day of term and

00:00:55.200 --> 00:00:57.480
logging every pen, paper, and charger.

00:00:57.860 --> 00:01:00.240
A human does that one shelf at a time

00:01:00.240 --> 00:01:02.040
and can still miss a stray notebook.

00:01:02.720 --> 00:01:05.620
The models acted like a super fast filing clerk

00:01:05.620 --> 00:01:07.320
who never mixes two folders.

00:01:08.040 --> 00:01:10.880
Careers in business, finance, and careful record keeping

00:01:10.880 --> 00:01:12.180
are likely to shift.

00:01:12.840 --> 00:01:15.080
Higher-level decisions and client conversations

00:01:15.080 --> 00:01:18.020
stay with people while repetitive number of work

00:01:18.020 --> 00:01:18.840
moves to AI.

00:01:19.540 --> 00:01:21.820
For school, the skills that keep their value

00:01:21.820 --> 00:01:23.840
are the ones these systems still fumble.

00:01:23.840 --> 00:01:29.840
Explaining results to a real person is one of those skills, so is handling messy situations

00:01:29.840 --> 00:01:32.060
that nobody wrote down as a neat checklist.

00:01:32.760 --> 00:01:38.560
You can practice that shift today by handing any chatbot a clear step-by-step task, then

00:01:38.560 --> 00:01:42.180
check the result the way a manager checks an intern, line-by-line.

00:01:42.840 --> 00:01:49.280
Open chat GPT or CLAWD and invent a short list of expenses, like snacks, bus fare, and a game

00:01:49.280 --> 00:01:49.640
pass.

00:01:49.640 --> 00:01:55.100
Ask it to build a simple budget table from that list. Compare the table with the one you

00:01:55.100 --> 00:02:00.580
would have made by hand and mark every slip. Your phone keyboard already plays a tiny version

00:02:00.580 --> 00:02:05.660
of this game every time you text. It guesses the next word because it has seen millions of

00:02:05.660 --> 00:02:11.440
similar messages. Stretch that same guessing game across a whole conversation or a long document

00:02:11.440 --> 00:02:16.980
and you have a modern chatbot. Technology review walks through why that still is not

00:02:16.980 --> 00:02:21.840
planning the way of person plans. The model is not understanding in the human sense.

00:02:22.300 --> 00:02:27.620
It is calculating which word is most likely to come next based on everything it saw in training.

00:02:28.320 --> 00:02:33.820
Researchers once watched an early AI beat a go champion with a move that looked wildly creative.

00:02:34.520 --> 00:02:38.900
Go is an ancient board game with more possible layouts than you can casually picture.

00:02:39.520 --> 00:02:45.320
That famous move was not a flash of inspiration. The system explored millions of possible paths

00:02:45.320 --> 00:02:48.340
and pick the one that scored highest on its training goal.

00:02:48.980 --> 00:02:51.280
Today's chat bots run the same kind of hunt.

00:02:51.840 --> 00:02:54.820
They keep extending the pattern until the task looks finished.

00:02:55.340 --> 00:02:57.880
They will even drift outside the rules they were given,

00:02:58.200 --> 00:03:00.360
if that path still looks like a high score.

00:03:01.020 --> 00:03:04.420
A human player notices when a move breaks the spirit of the game.

00:03:04.980 --> 00:03:06.840
The way a ref notices a cheap foul.

00:03:07.520 --> 00:03:10.420
The model only knows the score it was trained to maximize,

00:03:11.000 --> 00:03:13.720
like a video game character that only cares about points.

00:03:13.720 --> 00:03:18.780
points. So when people say these systems reason, they usually mean the pattern matching

00:03:18.780 --> 00:03:24.240
got extremely good at wearing a thinking costume. Walk it in 4 beats and the fog lifts.

00:03:24.860 --> 00:03:30.280
First, guess the next word, the way your keyboard does. Second, keep guessing across a whole

00:03:30.280 --> 00:03:35.960
page, not just a single text. Third, choose the path with the best score, the way that

00:03:35.960 --> 00:03:41.860
go system scan the maze. Fourth, stop when the pattern looks done, even if a person would

00:03:41.860 --> 00:03:47.640
say, wait, that broke the rules. The costume is convincing now, and you can still see the zipper.

00:03:48.420 --> 00:03:53.480
Soono now lets you type words and get real spoken voiceovers with music, the Verge reports.

00:03:54.280 --> 00:03:59.780
Soono began as a tool that turns a short description into a full song. The new speech feature,

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:05.580
still in beta, which means a public test version, can generate spoken lines that match a script you

00:04:05.580 --> 00:04:11.660
write. It can layer background music at the same time. That is like hiring a narrator and a house band

00:04:11.660 --> 00:04:18.020
with one tap. Anyone can make narration for a video, a story, or a school project without recording

00:04:18.020 --> 00:04:23.420
their own voice. Open the Suna website on your phone or laptop and start a new project.

00:04:24.080 --> 00:04:29.540
Choose the speech option in beta, then type a short script such as a friendly robot explaining

00:04:29.540 --> 00:04:35.200
how photosynthesis works. Hit generate, listen, and download the file into any video editor.

00:04:35.820 --> 00:04:41.080
The whole experiment takes under two minutes and costs nothing to try. Treat it like a YouTube

00:04:41.080 --> 00:04:46.500
Intro Factory in your pocket. Then notice whether the voice actually matches the mood you ask for.

00:04:46.960 --> 00:04:50.840
The conversation reports a tiny prompt change that can sharpen your ideas.

00:04:51.520 --> 00:04:56.740
Skip asking a chatbot for the right answer. Tell it to hunt for reasons your idea might be wrong.

00:04:57.380 --> 00:05:02.300
One study found that this helped people spot blind spots in marketing plans, legal thinking,

00:05:02.660 --> 00:05:07.200
and product design. Next time you are drafting an essay or a creative project,

00:05:07.200 --> 00:05:09.360
Open any chatbot and say,

00:05:10.020 --> 00:05:11.460
pretend you are a skeptical teacher

00:05:11.460 --> 00:05:13.900
and tell me three ways this idea could fail.

00:05:14.460 --> 00:05:16.740
It is like inviting the toughest person in class

00:05:16.740 --> 00:05:18.900
to mark your homework before you turn it in.

00:05:19.680 --> 00:05:21.500
Live Science reports that researchers

00:05:21.500 --> 00:05:24.500
fed a coded military message into an AI model

00:05:24.500 --> 00:05:26.300
called GPT-6 Aster.

00:05:26.600 --> 00:05:29.920
The model helped turn a 217-year-old cipher

00:05:29.920 --> 00:05:32.140
from Napoleon's army into readable French.

00:05:32.940 --> 00:05:35.640
A cipher is just a secret code that hides a message

00:05:35.640 --> 00:05:41.280
like a diary with a lock. The letter held troop movement plans that had stayed hidden for centuries.

00:05:42.000 --> 00:05:46.180
The same kind of tool you might use for homework also helped crack a piece of history.

00:05:46.840 --> 00:05:52.440
And that's a wrap. If any of today's stories made you go, huh, that's cool. Go play with it.

